  "product_name": "PRP Simulation Container",
  "registry_version": 6,
  "status": "experimental",
  "maintainer": "Arctic3D AB",
  "maintainer_email": "ceo@arctic3d.xyz",
  "usage": "PRP NODE\n========\n\nIEC 62439-3 RedBox. The node bridges a SAN (eth2, interlink) onto two\nPRP LANs (eth0 = LAN A, eth1 = LAN B) or onto an HSR ring. Frames are\nduplicated with an RCT trailer (PRP) or HSR tag, deduplicated on\nreceive, and supervised with 0x88FB frames.\n\nThe console shows a full-screen TUI (stats, settings, debug log)\nwhen stdout is a terminal; otherwise a periodic one-line status.\nThe telnet aux console on port 5000 gives a shell. Set PRP_NO_TUI=1\nto force the plain status output.\n\nENVIRONMENT VARIABLES\n---------------------\n\nSet these per node (right-click the node, Configure, General,\nEnvironment). They override the values in the config file.\n\n  PRP_ROLE           (default prp-san)\n    prp-san  = SAN into two PRP LANs (legacy name: redbox)\n    hsr-san  = SAN into an HSR ring\n    hsr-prp  = HSR ring into one PRP LAN (see coupling below)\n    hsr-hsr  = two HSR rings coupled (QuadBox; see below)\n\n  HSR_PRP_ID         (default 1)\n    NetId of the coupled PRP network, 1-6. Both hsr-prp nodes that\n    couple the same PRP network to the ring must share this value.\n\n  HSR_LAN_ID         (default A)\n    Which PRP LAN the hsr-prp interlink attaches to: A or B.\n\n  PRP_PRP_ID         (default 0)\n    PRP network ID; 0 = auto-derive a unique ID from the hostname.\n\n  PRP_LAN_A_IP       (default none)\n    Optional static IPv4 address in CIDR form on LAN A, e.g. 10.1.0.10/24.\n\n  PRP_LAN_B_IP       (default none)\n    Optional static IPv4 address in CIDR form on LAN B.\n\n  PRP_INTERLINK_IP   (default none)\n    Optional static IPv4 address in CIDR form on the interlink/SAN.\n\n  DEBUG_FRAMES       (default unset)\n    Set to 1 to log every forwarded or dropped frame decision.\n\n  PRP_CONFIG_PATH    (default /etc/prp/config.yaml)\n    Path to an alternative config file.\n\n  PRP_NO_TUI         (default unset)\n    Set to 1 to disable the full-screen TUI on the console.\n\nHSR-PRP COUPLING (DUAL REDBOX)\n------------------------------\n\nTo connect an HSR ring to a PRP network, place two hsr-prp nodes on the\nsame ring:\n\n  node 1: HSR_LAN_ID=A, attached to PRP LAN A\n  node 2: HSR_LAN_ID=B, attached to PRP LAN B\n  both:   same HSR_PRP_ID (e.g. 1)\n\nThe PathId (NetId, LanId) in the HSR tag prevents frames from one PRP\nLAN being reinjected onto the other (IEC 62439-3 COR1 5.2.2.3.1).\n\nHSR-HSR (QUADBOX, TWO RINGS)\n----------------------------\n\nTo connect two HSR rings, place two hsr-hsr nodes joined by their\ninterlink ports:\n\n  node A: eth0/eth1 on ring 1, eth2 = interlink to node B\n  node B: eth0/eth1 on ring 2, eth2 = interlink to node A\n\nFrames cross from one ring to the other over the interlink with the\nsequence number preserved, so both rings form one redundancy domain.\n\nVERIFICATION\n------------\n\n  - Add a publisher on one side and a subscriber on the other. The\n    subscriber console shows unique= and dupes= counts; zero duplicates\n    proves exactly-once delivery across the PRP/HSR network.\n  - Disconnect LAN A (or one ring link) while pinging; traffic keeps\n    flowing on the remaining path.",
  "docker": {
    "adapters": 3,
    "image": "ghcr.io/grymme/prp-sim:latest",
    "console_type": "telnet",
    "environment": "PRP_ROLE=redbox"
  },
  "symbols": {
    "default": "symbols/prp-node.svg"
  }
}
